1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,400
We began with why Java exists and how a
hello world runs on a JVM. We walked
language, APIs, concurrency, the JVM's
memory and execution machinery, design
vocabulary, Spring, and distributed
systems.

2
00:00:14,519 --> 00:00:26,600
The last question is not another feature.
It is whether you would sleep the night
after shipping. Production-ready means
observable, recoverable, capacity-tested,
and owned — not merely running.

3
00:00:26,719 --> 00:00:28,559
Look at this text for a moment.

4
00:00:28,679 --> 00:00:39,439
Observable means you can explain a bad
minute with metrics, traces, and logs —
and that the JVM toolkit from earlier
episodes is reachable when the bad minute
is a heap climb or a stuck thread.

5
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:45,880
Recoverable means timeouts, retries done
safely, circuit breakers, and a rollback
you have rehearsed.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:55,519
Capacity-tested means load tests that look
like production and SLOs you actually
measure — you know the RPS at which p99
breaks and what you will do.

7
00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:10,239
Owned means names on runbooks and pages,
not a distribution list nobody reads. A
perfect dashboard with no human who can
roll back is decoration. A runbook that
has never been exercised in a game day is
fiction.

8
00:01:10,359 --> 00:01:22,879
SLOs and alerts come first. What does
healthy mean in user terms, and who gets
paged when error budgets burn? Write
alerts in the language of user pain and
attach the runbook link in the page
itself.

9
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:35,159
Review noisy alerts like flaky tests — fix
or delete. Dashboards that answer
questions beat vanity charts: is success
rate healthy, which dependency hurts, are
we near saturation?

10
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:45,700
Three panels may be enough for a small
service; more panels can wait until a
question appears that they answer.
Rollback plan before deploy: can you
revert quickly when the new version lies?

11
00:01:45,879 --> 00:01:55,280
Include database migrations —
expand/contract so rollback does not
require reverse mythology. Feature flags
can help when coordinated with the same
ownership model.

12
00:01:55,399 --> 00:02:08,399
Shipping without rollback turns every
deploy into a one-way door. Prefer doors
with handles on both sides. Dependency
limits — timeouts, bulkheads, circuit
breakers — keep one slow friend from
taking you down.

13
00:02:08,519 --> 00:02:17,759
Every added client is an added way to die.
Those limits are part of the API of a good
citizen service, not optional polish. Walk
a launch gate.

14
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:31,560
Before traffic: SLO written, dashboard
live, alert routed, runbook linked,
rollback tested in staging, load test
signed off, dependency timeouts set,
security checks on.

15
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:47,200
After traffic: watch error budgets for the
first hours, keep a human near the
rollback button, compare JVM and HTTP
metrics to the baseline. That is
production readiness as a practice, not a
poster. No owner for 3 a.m.

16
00:02:47,319 --> 00:02:57,519
is a management defect as much as an
engineering one. Rotate ownership,
compensate it, train it. Production
readiness is socio-technical.

17
00:02:57,639 --> 00:03:03,639
The series taught mechanisms; this
capstone insists on people and practice
around those mechanisms.

18
00:03:03,759 --> 00:03:15,039
When a shiny new platform claims to make
you production-ready automatically, ask it
those four questions — observable,
recoverable, capacity-tested, owned — and
listen for silence.

19
00:03:15,159 --> 00:03:27,759
This series does not end because Java
ends. It ends because you now have a chain
of thought: situation, problem, mechanism,
evidence, trade-off. We refused syllabus
dumps.

20
00:03:27,879 --> 00:03:38,759
We started from situations — portability
pain, leaking references, cargo-cult
flags, double charges on retry — and let
Java's answers arrive as necessities.

21
00:03:38,879 --> 00:03:50,439
Carry that method into frameworks we never
named and into systems you will invent.
The final test remains: if you remove the
headings, does your explanation still
sound like one engineer thinking with a
teammate?

22
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:58,180
If yes, you learned the real curriculum.
There is no Episode Eighty-Six bridge.
Your bridge is the next production system
you touch.

23
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:08,900
Take the chain of thought with you —
situation, problem, question, mechanism,
evidence, trade-off — and the Java Story
keeps teaching after the narration ends.

24
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:12,300
Welcome to building and operating Java
systems with eyes open.
